WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.509
"Is Six Hours of Sleep Enough?"

00:00:11.509 --> 00:00:14.470
Conventional wisdom has it
that over the last 50 years

00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:18.289
sleep duration has declined
in parallel with the increasing

00:00:18.289 --> 00:00:21.090
prevalence of obesity,
suggesting that an epidemic

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:25.269
of sleep loss is associated with
the epidemic of weight gain.

00:00:25.269 --> 00:00:29.179
Now, we have triple digit streaming
TV channels, smartphones,

00:00:29.179 --> 00:00:31.730
and tablets to keep us entertained
well into the night.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:35.110
"The hurry and excitement
of modern life is quite correctly

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:38.899
held to be responsible
for much of the insomnia,"

00:00:38.899 --> 00:00:41.110
concluded one medical
journal editorial.

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:46.400
But that was an editorial
published in 1894.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:49.370
Are we really sleeping
that much less?

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:52.609
Since 1905, sleep duration
in children and adolescents

00:00:52.609 --> 00:00:55.280
has declined by a little
over an hour a night.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:59.860
However, child labor wasn't
outlawed until 1938 though,

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:02.730
and so part of the explanation
may be due to the exhaustion

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:05.670
of sweating it out in the mines,
farms, and factories

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:08.110
in the early part of the last century.

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:10.930
Since 1970, youth sleep duration
has only declined about

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:12.300
15 minutes per night,

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:17.060
and it's not clear if sleep duration
in adults has changed much at all.

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:21.380
Based on 168 studies of objective
measurements of sleep duration,

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:23.020
instead of just self-report,

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:26.390
sleep duration in adults --
objective total sleep time --

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:29.770
has't changed much since 1960.

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:32.220
Since 2003, average sleep
duration in the United States

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.950
may have even gone up.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:36.150
Now, of course, just because
we don't have evidence

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:39.659
that there's been a growing
epidemic of sleep deprivation

00:01:39.659 --> 00:01:42.719
that doesn't necessarily mean
we're getting enough sleep.

00:01:42.719 --> 00:01:45.840
Maybe we weren't getting enough
sleep 50 years ago either,

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.930
or since Edison's lightbulbs,

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:52.310
or since candles were
invented 5,000 years ago.

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:55.700
How might we determine
the optimal sleep duration?

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.250
One way would be to study
millions of people and see

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:02.600
how many hours a night is
associated with the longest lifespan.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.380
Sleep is a great mystery.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:08.940
A trait shared across animal species,
sleep must be of vital importance

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:13.709
to survive natural selection pressures
to eliminate such a vulnerable state.

00:02:13.709 --> 00:02:16.990
Indeed, cringe-worthy
experiments have shown that

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:22.500
keeping animals awake long enough
is fatal within 11 to 32 days.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.690
One of the functions of sleep that
has been elucidated in recent years

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:28.280
is the clearance of toxic
waste substances

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.050
that build up during the day

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:33.540
through a newly discovered
drainage system in the brain.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:35.680
This could help explain why
those who routinely get

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:39.080
less than seven hours of sleep
at night may be at increased risk

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:42.810
of developing cognitive
disorders such as dementia.

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:46.230
Even a single all-nighter can
cause a significant increase

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:49.440
in beta amyloid accumulation
in critical brain areas,

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:54.830
a gummy substance implicated in the
development of Alzheimer's disease.

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:57.620
The lowest risk for developing
cognitive impairment was found

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:00.050
for those getting seven to
eight hours of sleep a night,

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:04.060
which is the same optimal range
found for diabetes risk,

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:07.420
based on 36 studies following
more than a million people.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:10.280
The increased risk associated
with getting only six hours a night

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.420
compared to seven or eight
is comparable

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:17.180
to the increase in diabetes risk
linked to physical inactivity.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:20.930
For death from all causes combined,
there's been more than 50 studies

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:25.090
following hundreds of thousands
of people for up to 34 years.

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:27.980
Sleeping too short and too long
are both associated

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:29.519
with cutting one's life short,

00:03:29.519 --> 00:03:33.400
with the apparent sweet spot
at seven hours a night.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.840
Seven hours may seem short,
but that may actually be

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:37.950
what's natural for our species.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:41.180
Scientists studied three isolated
pre-industrial societies

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:44.620
across two continents and
found a surprising uniformity.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:47.060
Despite no electric lighting
or gadgets,

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:48.840
they stayed up until
about three hours

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:52.510
after sunset and then
typically rose before dawn,

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:55.010
accumulating about a solid
six and a half hours of sleep

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:58.760
out of about seven and
a half hours in "bed."

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:02.720
A mechanism by which excess sleep
might be harmful remains elusive,

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:05.650
and so the association between
increased risk of death and disease

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:09.590
and sleeping nine or more hours
a night has been largely dismissed

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:11.560
as implausible.

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:12.980
Maybe its reverse causation,

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:16.400
sickness leading to more time
in bed instead of vice-versa,

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:17.620
or confounding factors

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:19.690
such as employment status.

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:21.810
After all, who gets to sleep in?

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:23.680
Those without a job to get to.

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:26.720
However, there is experimental
evidence showing negative

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:28.900
health effects from insufficient sleep.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:30.180
So in terms of sleeping

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:36.000
in the sweet spot, aim for at least
seven hours of regular sleep a day.

